  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: authorName(Astro.url.pathname),
    section: "Cleaning & Shop Tools",
    tags: [
      "best electric pressure washer",
      "plug-in pressure washer",
      "cordless pressure washer",
      "brushless pressure washer",
      "pressure washer for car",
      "electric pressure washer for deck"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <header data-hero class="bg-[#F4F6F8] border-b border-gray-200">
      <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-10 pb-8 lg:pt-14 lg:pb-10">
        <p class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-2 gap-y-1 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest m-0 mb-4">
          <span class="text-[#D93A00]">Buyer's Guide</span>
          <span class="text-gray-300" aria-hidden="true">&bull;</span>
          <span class="text-gray-500">Updated {dates.display}</span>
        </p>
        <h1 class="text-[2.1rem] leading-[1.15] sm:text-5xl font-black tracking-tight text-gray-900 m-0">
          The 6 Best Electric Pressure Washers for the House, the Car and the Deck
        </h1>
        <p class="text-lg lg:text-xl text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mt-5 mb-0">
          No fuel, no pull cord, no fumes. These plug-in and battery machines handle the jobs most homeowners actually do, and this guide is honest about the ones they cannot.
        </p>
        <!-- Author & Meta -->
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mt-7">
          <AuthorAvatar size={40} />
          <div class="min-w-0">
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 m-0">Written by <a href="/authors/alex-rivers/" class="font-bold text-gray-900 no-underline hover:underline">Alex Rivers</a></p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 m-0">Home Improvement Editor &middot; Updated {dates.display}</p>
          </div>
          <SocialProof users="" />
        </div>
        <div class="flex items-center gap-4 mt-6">
          <TrustBadge />
          <span class="flex-1 h-px bg-gray-300"></span>
        </div>
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mt-3 mb-0">
          We judge every product on its spec sheet, its label and what owners report over time, not on the marketing. No paid placements, and commission never sets a ranking. <a href="/how-we-test/" class="font-bold text-gray-900 underline">Read how we pick</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-8">
      <details open class="group bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl px-5 pt-4 pb-2">
        <summary class="flex items-center gap-3 pb-3 cursor-pointer list-none [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
          <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-gray-500" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path stroke-linecap="round" d="M9 6h11M9 12h11M9 18h11M4.5 6h.01M4.5 12h.01M4.5 18h.01" /></svg>
          <span class="font-bold text-gray-900">In This Guide</span>
          <span class="text-xs font-semibold text-gray-400">12 sections</span>
          <svg class="w-5 h-5 ml-auto text-gray-500 transition-transform group-open:rotate-180" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M6 15l6-6 6 6" /></svg>
        </summary>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-x-6">
              <a href="#top-picks" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">1.</span><span>Top Picks</span></a>
              <a href="#outlet-limit" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">2.</span><span>The Outlet Limit</span></a>
              <a href="#motors" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">3.</span><span>Motor Types</span></a>
              <a href="#cords-water" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">4.</span><span>Cords, Hoses and Water</span></a>
              <a href="#what-it-cleans" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">5.</span><span>What It Cleans Well</span></a>
              <a href="#cordless" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">6.</span><span>Battery Models</span></a>
              <a href="#car-deck-siding" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">7.</span><span>Cars, Decks and Siding</span></a>
              <a href="#soap-foam" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">8.</span><span>Soap and Foam</span></a>
              <a href="#care" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">9.</span><span>Care and Storage</span></a>
              <a href="#cost" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">10.</span><span>Cost and Value</span></a>
              <a href="#mistakes" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">11.</span><span>Common Mistakes</span></a>
              <a href="#faq" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">12.</span><span>FAQ</span></a>
        </nav>
      </details>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          An electric pressure washer is the right tool for most homes, and the wrong one for a few. If your list is a car every month, a deck in spring, vinyl siding, a grill and some patio furniture, a good plug-in does all of it quietly and starts every time. If your list is dominated by a long concrete driveway, no electric will make that fast, because a household outlet caps how much water the pump can move. This guide ranks six electric machines, explains the motor and circuit limits that separate good ones from bad ones, and covers technique for the surfaces electric machines are best at.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />


        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The 6 Best Electric Pressure Washers, Ranked
          </h2>

          <p>
            Picks are based on manufacturer ratings for pressure and flow, motor type, frame and storage design, fitting standards and warranty, along with recurring themes in owner feedback about durability and pump life. We weighted machines that hold up to regular household use. No hands-on testing is claimed, and no brand paid for placement.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} index={index} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="outlet-limit" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What a Household Outlet Can Power
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every plug-in pressure washer is boxed in by the same constraint. A standard 120-volt, 15-amp circuit can safely supply about 1,800 watts continuously, and most machines are designed to draw 13 to 15 amps. That energy has to be split between pressure and flow. A pump can push a small amount of water at high pressure or more water at moderate pressure, but not a lot of both. That is why plug-ins top out around 1.1 to 1.8 GPM no matter how big the PSI number printed on the side is.
          </p>

          <p>
            In practice, this means two electric machines with very different pressure ratings often clean at similar speeds. A 3,000 PSI unit at 1.1 GPM and a 2,000 PSI unit at 1.5 GPM deliver similar total cleaning energy, measured as pressure times flow. The higher-pressure machine is better at blasting a stubborn spot; the higher-flow machine is better at rinsing a big wall or a car. Choose based on which of those describes your work.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">The Plug-In Envelope</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">~1,800 W</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Continuous limit, 15 A circuit</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">1.1-1.8</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical GPM range</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">1,700-3,000</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical rated PSI</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">2,000-3,500</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical cleaning units</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Pay attention to whether figures are labeled max or rated. Peak pressure is a spike the pump can reach briefly; peak flow is measured with no nozzle restriction. Real working figures are lower, sometimes considerably. Machines certified to the industry standards used by CETA or the PWMA publish rated performance you can compare more fairly. When a budget box lists only max numbers, treat them as optimistic and compare on price, motor and build instead.
          </p>

          <p>
            Share the circuit with nothing else. Running a pressure washer on the same garage circuit as a freezer or a compressor invites nuisance trips and voltage drop that makes the motor work harder. If your outdoor outlet trips often under load, the machine is telling you something about the wiring, and that is a job for an electrician rather than a heavier extension cord.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="motors" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Universal, Induction and Brushless Motors
          </h2>

          <p>
            The motor decides how loud the machine is and, often, how long it lasts. Universal motors, found on most budget plug-ins, spin very fast and use carbon brushes to carry current to the spinning armature. They are light and cheap, which is why they power most machines under about $200. They are also loud, run hot, and the brushes wear, so these machines tend to have shorter service lives, especially under long sessions.
          </p>

          <p>
            Induction motors have no brushes and turn more slowly. They are heavier and cost more, but they run cooler, are noticeably quieter, and are commonly expected to last much longer. Machines like the Karcher K5 and the Greenworks Pro use them, and they are the better choice for anyone who washes regularly. Brushless DC motors, like the one in the Ryobi RY142300, get many of the same benefits with lighter weight and good efficiency.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
